Miss Potts, and I don't know anything at all about his comings and goings.

00:32:40.840 --> 00:32:41.820
Doesn't he answer the bell?

00:32:42.220 --> 00:32:42.940
Oh, he doesn't.

00:32:43.220 --> 00:32:45.560
I was just going in. Cleaning day today, you know.

00:32:46.060 --> 00:32:50.320
I suppose he's lying stiff and the place all clubbed up with gin bottles.

00:32:50.820 --> 00:32:52.200
Let's go in and have a look around, Captain.

00:32:52.840 --> 00:32:57.660
As the tenant gets by me, though, he wasn't so bad until he lost his job.

00:32:58.040 --> 00:32:59.920
Then he started acting queer like...

00:33:00.600 --> 00:33:02.000
See, I told you.

00:33:03.740 --> 00:33:05.830
And they done law to him.

00:33:05.950 --> 00:33:10.270
I said, Mr. Chalmers, my bungalow court isn't an alcoholic ward.

00:33:11.170 --> 00:33:12.950
As I thought, drunk again.

00:33:13.290 --> 00:33:14.650
Well, this is the end.

00:33:14.850 --> 00:33:16.050
This is no key to cure.

00:33:16.350 --> 00:33:17.070
Out he goes.

00:33:17.680 --> 00:33:19.560
I'm sick and tired of his carrying's on.

00:33:22.170 --> 00:33:22.830
The fellow's dead.

00:33:23.180 --> 00:33:23.380
Dead?

00:33:24.970 --> 00:33:25.890
He didn't think of me.

00:33:26.050 --> 00:33:27.110
No, not him.

00:33:33.930 --> 00:33:35.530
Did I have Chalmers spotted?

00:33:36.260 --> 00:33:38.710
All I did was ask myself, who done it?

00:33:39.050 --> 00:33:41.260
Poison, all right. Familiar smelling stuff, too.

00:33:45.590 --> 00:33:47.770
Guy, I drink anything that wasn't too thick to chew.

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:02.680
Well, I guess this about washes up the case, eh, Mr. Drew?

00:34:02.980 --> 00:34:04.810
Looks like suicide to you, eh, Gully?

00:34:05.210 --> 00:34:05.530
Sure.

00:34:07.700 --> 00:34:09.080
I guess Sheehan would think so, too.

00:34:09.360 --> 00:34:11.380
Uh, he may be dumb, but he ain't blind.

00:34:11.520 --> 00:34:12.420
He's not as dumb as he looks.

00:34:13.210 --> 00:34:14.190
Do you wear a wristwatch, Gully?

00:34:14.780 --> 00:34:14.940
Huh?

00:34:15.500 --> 00:34:16.520
Do you wear a watch on your wrist?

00:34:17.620 --> 00:34:21.000
Well, you see, it's a present from the wife.

00:34:21.790 --> 00:34:22.810
I gotta wear it.

00:34:22.930 --> 00:34:24.230
Why do you wear it on your left wrist?

00:34:25.310 --> 00:34:28.050
Well, I use my right hand the most, because I'm right-handed.

00:34:28.330 --> 00:34:29.890
You ever try to write with it on your right...

00:34:31.280 --> 00:34:31.580
No.

00:34:33.300 --> 00:34:37.460
Neither did Chalmers, which makes this note a very interesting piece of fiction.

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:42.340
a gully come here take a look in here now what do you see a bottle of gin and a piece of ice

00:35:42.340 --> 00:35:50.430
what else use your powers of observation oh you mean that piece of wooded lettuce in there don't

00:35:50.430 --> 00:35:54.790
you notice that little service door on the back has been left open whatever the ice men probably

00:35:54.790 --> 00:35:59.740
left it that way my wife says you can't trust those ice men anyway if the ice men had left that little

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:05.530
open when he put in the fresh cake there wouldn't be any ice there now oh i never thought of that

00:36:06.130 --> 00:36:29.840
let's have a look outside oh someone must have stood here a long time

00:36:30.320 --> 00:36:31.860
Them are fresh prints, ain't they?

00:36:33.250 --> 00:36:34.010
Step up there a minute.

00:36:35.600 --> 00:36:36.400
Look out for those cracks.

00:36:38.100 --> 00:36:38.460
Now what?

00:36:39.050 --> 00:36:39.690
Look through the window.

00:36:43.990 --> 00:36:45.050
You see Chalmers sitting there?

00:36:45.440 --> 00:36:46.460
Yeah, the door's open.

00:36:51.920 --> 00:36:53.260
Supposing someone stood here,

00:36:54.400 --> 00:36:56.520
and Dr. Chalmers' jazz box from this side,

00:36:59.280 --> 00:36:59.980
and then stood here.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:05.220
Yeah, and watched him drink his last drink through the window, what would you say?

00:37:05.620 --> 00:37:06.720
And then watched him die.

00:37:07.680 --> 00:37:09.800
Went in and wrote the note and put the pencil in his hand, huh?

00:37:10.140 --> 00:37:11.760
Yeah, only there's where he made his mistake.

00:37:12.270 --> 00:37:13.730
Put the pencil in the wrong hand.

00:37:14.660 --> 00:37:16.120
That's just what I was thinking.

00:37:19.890 --> 00:37:22.840
Gully, you know, if a car was parked here,

00:37:24.080 --> 00:37:25.880
it would entirely conceal that door and that window.

00:37:27.280 --> 00:37:27.560
Yeah?

00:37:39.500 --> 00:37:41.360
Then Chalmers didn't kill himself, huh?

00:37:41.560 --> 00:37:42.500
Let's leave that to Sheehan.

00:37:42.970 --> 00:37:43.930
I'm any good as a guesser.

00:37:43.970 --> 00:37:45.050
He ought to be here by now.

00:37:48.820 --> 00:37:49.880
Look over on the table.

00:37:50.420 --> 00:37:50.920
Yeah, huh?

00:37:51.830 --> 00:37:53.230
Don't let Sheehan know what we found out.

00:37:53.410 --> 00:37:53.890
I got you.

00:37:54.540 --> 00:37:56.320
The captain got here 15 minutes ago.

00:37:56.580 --> 00:37:56.960
Captain?

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:03.350
What are you and that Keystone cop doing here?

00:38:03.950 --> 00:38:04.750
Now, looky here, Shane.

00:38:04.850 --> 00:38:06.210
You can't talk that way about me.

00:38:06.450 --> 00:38:09.030
Remember, I'm chief of the studio police, and I got rights.

00:38:09.390 --> 00:38:09.730
Aye, aye.

00:38:10.070 --> 00:38:11.090
Nature in the raw.

00:38:12.060 --> 00:38:13.860
Here's another opportunity for your guilt, Lieutenant.

00:38:14.060 --> 00:38:14.920
We've been waiting for you.

00:38:15.140 --> 00:38:17.960
Everybody around here seems to know more about police matters than the police.

00:38:18.140 --> 00:38:19.300
That's noble of you to admit it.

00:38:19.510 --> 00:38:21.370
Did you two loosens this touch anything around here?

00:38:21.530 --> 00:38:22.130
Nothing important.

00:38:22.330 --> 00:38:22.530
Good.

00:38:22.690 --> 00:38:23.410
Then clear out.

00:38:24.350 --> 00:38:26.030
Take that uniform moron with you.

00:38:27.280 --> 00:38:27.920
Come on.

00:38:32.430 --> 00:38:33.190
Suicide, huh?

00:38:35.900 --> 00:38:36.480
Looks like it.

00:38:36.940 --> 00:38:39.340
Well, I always thought it was Chalmers right from the start.

00:38:40.740 --> 00:38:41.900
Now who's the Keystone cop?

00:38:42.040 --> 00:38:43.800
He don't even know Chalmers was left-handed.

00:38:44.080 --> 00:38:44.540
Come on.

00:38:47.820 --> 00:38:48.480
Left-handed?

00:38:56.940 --> 00:38:57.240
Sure.

00:38:58.220 --> 00:38:59.500
That starts it all over again.

00:39:00.640 --> 00:39:06.180
I can remember when murders were nice, clean, simple things done with machine guns and meat axes.

00:39:07.220 --> 00:39:08.040
But this case?

00:39:14.890 --> 00:39:28.120
It used to be the trouble.

00:39:28.560 --> 00:39:29.200
Batteries did.

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:31.980
But didn't Miss Lane get a new battery just last week?

00:39:32.180 --> 00:39:33.600
Yeah, I don't understand it.

00:39:33.740 --> 00:39:34.520
It's bone dry.

00:39:35.040 --> 00:39:36.140
Liquid's all gone.

00:39:48.000 --> 00:39:54.110
Just as I thought we were right.

00:39:54.740 --> 00:39:55.140
Poison.

00:39:58.740 --> 00:39:59.620
Electro-ill.

00:40:01.150 --> 00:40:07.900
Electrolytic liquid, 38% distilled water, 33% electrolytic liquid, 7% by volume.

00:40:08.330 --> 00:40:10.650
The man set a teaspoon full on a glass will kill a man dead.

00:40:11.940 --> 00:40:12.720
Electrolytic acid.

00:40:12.950 --> 00:40:13.950
Did you say anything else?

00:40:14.600 --> 00:40:14.900
No.

00:40:15.260 --> 00:40:15.820
That was all.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:33.980
Hey, ain't them the same kind of tread that left the marks under Chalmers' window?

00:40:34.160 --> 00:40:37.620
Oh, there's probably a thousand cars in the Hollywood that have that make of tire.

00:40:38.330 --> 00:40:39.470
I never thought of that.

00:40:42.340 --> 00:40:46.470
Say, Gully, I want you to get me the key to Brent's dressing room,

00:40:46.530 --> 00:40:48.030
but don't let anybody know it's for me, see?

00:40:48.390 --> 00:40:50.670
Say, I'll get you the key to the city if you want it.

00:40:51.310 --> 00:40:53.930
I have a couple of things to do. I'll meet you here in half an hour.

00:40:54.410 --> 00:40:54.690
Okay.

00:41:00.380 --> 00:41:00.820
Who is it?

00:41:01.580 --> 00:41:02.460
Right, may I come in?

00:41:02.920 --> 00:41:03.560
Just a minute.

00:41:12.460 --> 00:41:13.260
Why all the locks?

00:41:14.260 --> 00:41:16.600
I was just going through some old letters and didn't want to be disturbed.

00:41:16.940 --> 00:41:17.520
Oh, I see.

00:41:18.050 --> 00:41:18.710
Where have you been?

00:41:19.080 --> 00:41:20.680
I've been waiting all afternoon for you.

00:41:20.900 --> 00:41:22.300
Oh, I've just been doing a little sleuthing.

00:41:23.840 --> 00:41:24.960
Marsha, you trust me, don't you?

00:41:26.400 --> 00:41:27.520
Why, yes, of course.

00:41:29.470 --> 00:41:29.970
You know I do anything.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:35.520
anything in the world for you, don't you? I believe you would, dear. Then, is there

00:41:35.520 --> 00:41:43.380
anything you know about the murder of Brent that you haven't told me? No. I've just come

00:41:43.380 --> 00:41:50.900
from Chalmers' place. He's dead. Chalmers? Poisoned. You mean murdered? But isn't that

00:41:50.900 --> 00:41:58.190
it's a good imitation of it. Oh, how awful. Whoever did it used electrolytic acid. Acid

00:41:58.190 --> 00:41:59.960
out of a battery. The unfortunate...

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:04.980
Part of the whole thing is that they used your car and the acid out of your battery.

00:42:06.120 --> 00:42:12.460
My car? That's why my car wouldn't start.

00:42:12.980 --> 00:42:16.770
Yes. I only told you this because I'm afraid Sheehan will find out and arrest you on suspicion.

00:42:17.490 --> 00:42:19.210
That would mean headlines and scandal.

00:42:19.390 --> 00:42:21.750
What do you want me to do?

00:42:23.110 --> 00:42:26.140
I want you to get out of here. Anyplace, just so Sheehan can't find you.

00:42:27.330 --> 00:42:28.290
That's running away.

00:42:28.430 --> 00:42:29.970
Only for a day or two, just to give me time.

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:36.260
time. I'll go to Mary's at Malibu. She'll help me. Oh, that's as well. Make it fast,

00:42:36.320 --> 00:42:39.680
will you? There's no knowing how close she is behind me. I'll telephone you when I get

00:42:39.680 --> 00:42:45.090
it all worked out. You may have a long beard by then, but I'll try and hurry. Bye-bye,

00:42:45.110 --> 00:42:45.310
sweet.

00:43:13.470 --> 00:43:17.300
Come in, John.

00:43:21.380 --> 00:43:23.260
I suppose you're surprised that I'm here, huh, Marcia?

00:43:23.900 --> 00:43:24.640
Frankly, yes.

00:43:25.860 --> 00:43:29.540
You haven't come to see me since Brent's accident.

00:43:30.600 --> 00:43:31.860
I should think you'd know why.

00:43:32.670 --> 00:43:33.270
I don't.

00:43:34.260 --> 00:43:37.200
I only know that I want to help you.

00:43:38.350 --> 00:43:39.570
I have all the help I need.

00:43:40.560 --> 00:43:42.530
Marcia, you mustn't treat me like this.

00:43:43.480 --> 00:43:45.680
I never said anything to you while Brent was alive.

00:43:46.790 --> 00:43:48.030
You should know how I feel.

00:43:50.040 --> 00:43:52.320
You've succeeded in making your feelings quite plain enough.

00:43:53.700 --> 00:43:57.370
Marcia, I'm very fond of you.

00:43:59.230 --> 00:43:59.970
Not the kind of fond of you.

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:02.850
I care about. Please go now, Leon.

00:44:03.170 --> 00:44:04.340
I'm in rather a hurry.

00:44:15.990 --> 00:44:19.360
Sure. Maybe someday you will come to me and ask

00:44:19.360 --> 00:44:22.270
for help. Maybe you will apologize

00:44:22.270 --> 00:44:24.490
to me for laughing about how I feel

00:44:24.490 --> 00:44:28.150
for you. Perhaps Brent

00:44:28.150 --> 00:44:29.790
wasn't killed for nothing.

00:44:48.720 --> 00:44:51.180
No matter how smart they are, they always slip up.

00:44:57.840 --> 00:44:58.340
What's your name?

00:44:59.170 --> 00:44:59.670
Pardon me.

00:45:01.690 --> 00:45:04.060
Hello? Yes, sir. I will.

00:45:05.310 --> 00:45:06.830
Wait a minute. Here she is now.

00:45:08.480 --> 00:45:11.120
Miss Lane, Mr. Steiner would like to see you in his office.

00:45:11.950 --> 00:45:12.450
Thank you.

00:45:15.580 --> 00:45:16.140
What's your name?

00:45:16.770 --> 00:45:17.690
Mr. Wheatcroft.

00:45:18.410 --> 00:45:22.340
Mr. Todd, please. Mr. Wheatcroft calling to see you.

00:45:23.540 --> 00:45:24.520
All right. Thank you.

00:45:28.880 --> 00:45:29.740
Pull down that blind.

00:45:39.600 --> 00:45:56.940
see Brent when he came in? I see everybody. What kind of a suit do you have on? There it is. Funny she and

00:45:56.940 --> 00:45:59.750
miss looking in here. You've been keeping them too busy.

00:46:00.760 --> 00:46:01.620
See what those letters are.

00:46:05.890 --> 00:46:07.430
Here's one from a dame named Agnes.

00:46:08.690 --> 00:46:09.270
Agnes who?

00:46:09.810 --> 00:46:10.920
She ain't that dumb.

00:46:11.880 --> 00:46:12.560
She don't say much.

00:46:13.320 --> 00:46:15.760
Except that she can't live without him.

00:46:16.540 --> 00:46:17.180
Married, too.

00:46:18.620 --> 00:46:20.220
Says her husband don't understand her.

00:46:21.680 --> 00:46:23.380
Well, that's that old, old story.

00:46:23.660 --> 00:46:24.520
Two men and a woman.

00:46:25.360 --> 00:46:26.740
The internal triangle.

00:46:26.940 --> 00:46:29.980
What kind of a place of...

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:34.210
the clip side in. Oh, you know, one of those weekend places with all the Mr. Smiths and

00:46:34.210 --> 00:46:40.600
Mr. Jones and Mr. Browns. Brent wouldn't go there alone, would he? Not him. How far is

00:46:40.600 --> 00:46:45.270
ocean view from here? A couple hours north on the coast road. Well, I guess that's a

00:46:45.270 --> 00:46:52.650
stop. Why? I'm beginning to think there's a woman in the case. No. Mr. Drew, this case

00:46:52.650 --> 00:46:56.670
has become uncomplicated. Even for me. Come on.

00:47:02.720 --> 00:47:05.260
Gee, it's 4.30. I got to check in on my next...

00:47:05.730 --> 00:47:07.960
Listen, don't go away without me, will you?

00:47:09.670 --> 00:47:11.490
All right, Gully, I'll pick you up the gate when I go.

00:47:12.810 --> 00:47:14.310
Mr. Drew, I've been looking for you.

00:47:14.710 --> 00:47:17.410
Miss Lane wants to see you. She's in Mr. Steiner's office.

00:47:17.980 --> 00:47:18.440
Oh, thanks.

00:47:18.950 --> 00:47:19.310
Yes, sir.

00:47:28.620 --> 00:47:29.400
Right in, Mr. Drew.

00:47:30.380 --> 00:47:32.020
When did you find out your car wouldn't start?

00:47:32.220 --> 00:47:33.140
About an hour ago.

00:47:35.670 --> 00:47:39.290
Well, with all your dabbling, you didn't throw us off.

00:47:39.720 --> 00:47:43.800
You've thrown yourself off if you think Miss Lane had anything to do with Chalmers or Brent either.

00:47:44.580 --> 00:47:45.940
All right, all right.

00:47:46.210 --> 00:47:49.410
Just sit down there and keep quiet.

00:47:52.420 --> 00:47:53.400
Where did you go this afternoon?

00:47:54.030 --> 00:47:54.670
I went downtown.

00:47:55.110 --> 00:47:55.750
In your car?

00:47:56.120 --> 00:47:56.440
Yeah.

00:47:56.660 --> 00:47:57.340
Where did you park it?

00:47:57.920 --> 00:47:59.970
On the street, in front of Elf.

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:05.960
the flower shop. I ordered some flowers from Mr. Brent's funeral. Then where did you go? You went

00:48:05.960 --> 00:48:10.320
to Chalmers' house, didn't you? Wait a minute. I won't stand for this. You can't trick Miss Lane

00:48:10.320 --> 00:48:16.110
into answers and have little boy Blue over there take him down. Now keep quiet, Drew. You're trying

00:48:16.110 --> 00:48:19.750
to get at the bottom of this. Oh, no, you're not. She is trying to force Miss Lane into admissions

00:48:19.750 --> 00:48:24.880
of things she's never done. She might as well talk here as downtown. I've got a warrant for her.

00:48:25.290 --> 00:48:29.600
For what? Suspicion of murder. What's your theory? It's as simple as ABC.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:31.300
Yes, it would be if you thought of it.

00:48:33.090 --> 00:48:35.570
Chalmers killed Brent because he hated him and she offered him money.

00:48:36.120 --> 00:48:37.820
Then she got frightened and killed Chalmers,

00:48:38.030 --> 00:48:39.830
thinking that he would get drunk and give it away.

00:48:40.450 --> 00:48:42.370
Every piece of evidence we've got points to her.

00:48:42.840 --> 00:48:44.640
It was her cigarette that blew up the hill,

00:48:45.080 --> 00:48:47.320
her car that made the tracks outside of Chalmers' house,

00:48:47.580 --> 00:48:49.820
and acid from her battery that killed him.

00:48:49.940 --> 00:48:51.060
Oh, that's a lot of bunk.

00:48:51.770 --> 00:48:53.050
I know, you've got to make an arrest.

00:48:53.170 --> 00:48:53.950
It's okay with you.

00:48:55.840 --> 00:48:58.330
Tyna, you can't sit there and let them arrest Marsha.

00:48:58.600 --> 00:48:59.980
Well, she has nothing more to do with these...

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:02.160
that Lincoln had to do with the burning of Rome.

00:49:02.550 --> 00:49:05.980
Mr. Drew, we don't know whether Lincoln burned Rome or not,

00:49:06.020 --> 00:49:08.990
and we don't care, but this man is a policeman,

00:49:09.320 --> 00:49:10.620
so let us hear what he has to say.

00:49:10.760 --> 00:49:12.840
If you're not paying you to quarrel with the police...

00:49:12.840 --> 00:49:14.200
Oh, I don't care if you pay me a dime.

00:49:14.810 --> 00:49:15.970
She and if you arrest Miss Lane now,

00:49:16.030 --> 00:49:17.510
there'll be a lot of nasty newspaper headlines.

00:49:17.710 --> 00:49:19.510
Hold off for 24 hours and I'll be responsible.

00:49:19.810 --> 00:49:21.470
Better than that, I'll prove that she's innocent.

00:49:21.570 --> 00:49:23.070
Are you making yourself ridiculous?

00:49:23.270 --> 00:49:24.350
I'm trying to protect one of your stars.

00:49:25.140 --> 00:49:27.190
Wait a minute, Steiner. Maybe he's right.

00:49:27.990 --> 00:49:29.980
What about this scandal? What about her...

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:32.400
box of his name. Ain't he got enough trouble here

00:49:32.400 --> 00:49:33.820
already without having Marsha arrested?

00:49:34.040 --> 00:49:37.160
Now, Mr. Grossmith, if you want to accept the responsibility,

00:49:37.460 --> 00:49:39.270
you can. But remember,

00:49:39.960 --> 00:49:43.000
this is a murder case. Two people

00:49:43.000 --> 00:49:45.860
are dead. And how do you know someone else

00:49:45.860 --> 00:49:48.580
won't be killed? For my part,

00:49:48.580 --> 00:49:50.380
I believe that the police should not be

00:49:50.380 --> 00:49:53.620
interfered with. Well, all I gotta

00:49:53.620 --> 00:49:56.120
say is that it's a terrible calamity.

00:49:57.820 --> 00:49:59.800
Let's go, Helica. I guess the

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:00.600
Explosion's over.

00:50:01.670 --> 00:50:04.450
Someone in this studio killed Miles Brenton Chalmers.

00:50:04.470 --> 00:50:06.790
And he's trying to pin it on Marsha Lane.

00:50:08.070 --> 00:50:09.430
But they won't get away with it.

00:50:10.440 --> 00:50:11.140
Come on, Miss Lane.

00:50:15.690 --> 00:50:17.810
Marsha, honey, I'm sorry.

00:50:18.700 --> 00:50:20.300
I'll get you out of this as soon as I can.

00:50:20.790 --> 00:50:21.830
I know you will, Frank.

